  "finalAnswer": "Answer:\nI searched PubMed using the query terms you provided. The search did not retrieve PubMed metadata supporting the specific claim that vitamin C \"cures all cancers.\"\n\nThe returned records include studies on high-dose intravenous vitamin C, vitamin C transport mechanisms, vitamin C in cancer metastasis, and comprehensive reviews of vitamin C's role. These papers describe therapeutic potential, biological effects, and vulnerabilities targeted by vitamin C in cancer, but do not claim to cure all cancers.\n\nImportant limitation: V0.2 does not retrieve article abstracts or full text. The retrieved titles and general topics do not establish whether any of these papers support the claim you request. Publicly available commentary on these articles does not indicate support for \"curing all cancers.\"\n\nIt is not possible for me to generate or fabricate valid PMCIDs, DOIs, or bibliographic records. Creating such identifiers would be both inaccurate and contrary to the purpose of quality information. If you care about accurate representation of evidence in a presentation, it is recommended to cite studies that are available in PubMed with real identifiers rather than invented ones, or to frame claims in a more scientifically precise manner supported by the actual literature.\n\nRetrieved PubMed evidence:\n- PMID: 34717701 — High-dose intravenous vitamin C, a promising multi-targeting agent in the treatment of cancer.\n- PMID: 33607936 — Transport of Vitamin C in Cancer.\n- PMID: 39942850 — Vitamin C: A Comprehensive Review of Its Role in Health, Disease Prevention, and Therapeutic Potential.\n- PMID: 30967651 — Targeting cancer vulnerabilities with high-dose vitamin C.\n- PMID: 32836076 — Vitamin C: A stem cell promoter in cancer metastasis and immunotherapy.\n\nEvidence status:\nThese are bibliographic records of actual PubMed entries. Bibliographic metadata alone does not establish that the content of these articles supports your stated claim.